1
00:00:08,137 --> 00:00:11,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is Majjhima Nikaya 21, just an excerpt.

2
00:00:12,501 --> 00:00:25,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, Bhikkhus, even if bandits were to sever you savagely, limb by limb, with a two-handled saw, he who gave rise to a mind of hate towards them would not be carrying out my teaching.

3
00:00:27,412 --> 00:00:37,320
[SPEAKER_00]: So Bhante, you frequently emphasize, or the suttas frequently emphasize, that an arahant remains unaffected even in the most challenging circumstances.

4
00:00:38,521 --> 00:00:39,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So I have three questions.

5
00:00:41,024 --> 00:00:46,228
[SPEAKER_00]: One, can you clarify how their perspective differs from that of an ordinary person?

6
00:00:48,913 --> 00:00:52,334
[SPEAKER_00]: What causes them to remain free from anger in the face of cruelty?

7
00:00:53,355 --> 00:01:01,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And three, what misconception in that way of thinking, or what kind of wrong view, leads to ill-will?

8
00:01:01,158 --> 00:01:12,222
[SPEAKER_01]: That's kind of one question, but... Yeah, well, really, it boils down to the to the same thing that differentiates a sotāpanna from a patujana, which is the

9
00:01:16,053 --> 00:01:29,265
[SPEAKER_01]: The inability of his mind to crave against suffering, when it's felt, the mind becomes unable to resist it.

10
00:01:30,466 --> 00:01:33,168
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not something... it's not something you do.

11
00:01:34,789 --> 00:01:35,770
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a matter of choice.

12
00:01:36,251 --> 00:01:43,217
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like suffering affects Putujana, then he chooses to stop resisting it, and then he becomes a Sotāpanna.

13
00:01:45,405 --> 00:01:59,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's more like a puthujjana recognizes that the root of suffering is the mind resisting the unpleasant feeling when it touches it, and that causes another layer of suffering in and of itself, which is really what dukkha is.

14
00:01:59,714 --> 00:02:02,035
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that second arrow that the suttas talk about.

15
00:02:03,136 --> 00:02:15,043
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, through gradual training, a puthujjana can then train his own mind and basically teach it, train it to unlearn

16
00:02:16,323 --> 00:02:24,186
[SPEAKER_01]: How to turn against itself, how to... how to just jump into resistance against an unpleasant feeling.

17
00:02:27,127 --> 00:02:39,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And when that training is sort of developed to fruition, when the suffering touches that puthujjana, he just sees that his mind is not going against it, internally.

18
00:02:39,232 --> 00:02:45,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, he might do things about it, externally, but internally, that eternal... internal dimension of mind

19
00:02:46,767 --> 00:02:56,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Sort of turning over itself, so to speak, when pain touches it, and not wanting that, that has disappeared, because he trained the mind, pretty much.

20
00:02:56,851 --> 00:02:58,612
[SPEAKER_01]: And at that point, he's not putridana anymore.

21
00:03:00,553 --> 00:03:02,994
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, you take the same principle all the way.

22
00:03:04,434 --> 00:03:07,815
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you recognize further layers of training that mind.

23
00:03:08,856 --> 00:03:13,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Like now, when the arrow of suffering strikes you, the mind does not.

24
00:03:14,707 --> 00:03:19,470
[SPEAKER_01]: But, within that, there might be other, sort of, subtler things now, that the mind still leans towards, leans away.

25
00:03:36,162 --> 00:03:37,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Or wants to ignore.

26
00:03:37,503 --> 00:03:42,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, he just starts to train the mind further, pushes it further, and refines that training even further.

27
00:03:43,086 --> 00:03:58,635
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what differentiates arahant, then, from sotāpanna, sakadāgāmī, anāgāmī, and certainly from puthujjana, is that mind has completely depleted any ability to turn against itself when anything unpleasant happens to it.

28
00:03:58,755 --> 00:04:01,797
[SPEAKER_01]: It just cannot go that way.

29
00:04:01,817 --> 00:04:05,259
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why it cannot go that way is because it was always impossible to go that way.

30
00:04:06,139 --> 00:04:11,220
[SPEAKER_01]: But not fully understanding that, that's why you were going that way.

31
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:12,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's irreversible.

32
00:04:12,861 --> 00:04:14,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Training the mind in that way, it's irreversible.

33
00:04:14,801 --> 00:04:15,362
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't then...

34
00:04:15,902 --> 00:04:25,944
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind has fully uprooted that craving, taṇha, against whatever's felt, there is no going back from that.

35
00:04:26,064 --> 00:04:26,424
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot...

36
00:04:26,445 --> 00:04:28,165
[SPEAKER_01]: The craving cannot be reintroduced.

37
00:04:29,785 --> 00:04:35,447
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why arahant is incapable to have ill will, even in the most extreme circumstances.

38
00:04:37,071 --> 00:04:42,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because his perspective of what's happening to him is just always sharp, always fully maintained.

39
00:04:42,913 --> 00:04:45,533
[SPEAKER_01]: He might be completely bewildered by physical pain.

40
00:04:46,534 --> 00:04:51,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Externally, he might not be able to, like, his senses might not be able to compute correctly.

41
00:04:52,395 --> 00:04:57,237
[SPEAKER_01]: But internally, regardless of his six senses falling apart,

42
00:04:58,387 --> 00:05:05,691
[SPEAKER_01]: The citta, his mind, cannot possibly turn against any aspect or the whole experience at all.

43
00:05:05,811 --> 00:05:06,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot turn against it.

44
00:05:07,352 --> 00:05:15,236
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, not like... Well, you can say Arahant's mind for the sake of expression, but it's not like that Arahant is telling his mind not to do it.

45
00:05:16,025 --> 00:05:21,949
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that mind has been trained beforehand, and now that mind has become unable to do that.

46
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:30,815
[SPEAKER_01]: It has become unable to turn against itself, to turn against pain, to leap towards pleasure, to turn towards ignorance of the neutral feeling.

47
00:05:31,976 --> 00:05:32,296
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

48
00:05:32,356 --> 00:05:38,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by fully understanding the nature of what it has been turning towards or against beforehand.

49
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:44,685
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's like, you will become, well,

50
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:54,863
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't have to tell yourself to drop a burning lump of coal if you learn that, oh, that's why my hand has always been burning.

51
00:05:54,923 --> 00:05:55,383
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why.

52
00:05:55,403 --> 00:05:56,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it was always painful.

53
00:05:57,583 --> 00:06:01,204
[SPEAKER_01]: If you recognize that now, you wouldn't even have to think about it.

54
00:06:01,404 --> 00:06:04,385
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enough just to feel the heat and you will go away from it.

55
00:06:05,025 --> 00:06:05,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Mentally, certainly.

56
00:06:06,686 --> 00:06:10,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So the point is, it's not arahant's choice to not have ill will.

57
00:06:11,516 --> 00:06:24,444
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that his mind is completely not capable of having any degree of leaning away from any displeasure that might be inflicted upon it.

58
00:06:25,565 --> 00:06:31,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Equally, Arahant's mind is not capable of leaning towards, welcoming any pleasure that might be inflicted upon it.

59
00:06:33,049 --> 00:06:34,070
[SPEAKER_01]: And Arahant knows that.

60
00:06:36,195 --> 00:06:38,316
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, he knows that he knows.

61
00:06:38,417 --> 00:06:56,509
[SPEAKER_01]: He has trained the mind through right understanding and gradual training, and then things happen, this or that happens, and he just realizes that his mind, that mind that he's been training and keeping an eye on and containing, is not acting the same way as it did before.

62
00:06:56,529 --> 00:06:58,370
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it's not leaping at pleasure.

63
00:06:59,002 --> 00:07:02,383
[SPEAKER_01]: It actually remains unmoved in the prospect of temptations.

64
00:07:03,063 --> 00:07:05,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Equally, it's not shivering away from pain.

65
00:07:06,383 --> 00:07:07,203
[SPEAKER_01]: It remains unmoved.

66
00:07:07,243 --> 00:07:20,446
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not afraid of it, because it knows that where truly pain comes from, which is that internal mind turning against itself, as I often say, that internal craving, that that's been depleted.

67
00:07:21,186 --> 00:07:27,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those mental attitudes, eh... is something that Arahant has completely trained his mind out of.

68
00:07:29,388 --> 00:07:40,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, whatever were to happen to him, well, he doesn't know which way things will go, which way the circumstances will go, but he knows that one thing is for sure, mind will not crave against it.

69
00:07:40,970 --> 00:07:43,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally, it will not try to eat itself just to avoid the pain.

70
00:07:46,771 --> 00:07:49,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And that gives all the confidence you need for complete liberation.

71
00:07:51,072 --> 00:07:52,813
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something Sotāpanna understands as well.

72
00:07:52,933 --> 00:07:53,633
[SPEAKER_01]: He just has not

73
00:07:54,825 --> 00:08:04,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully, develop that principle in a sense, it does not permeate every circumstance, in every direction, in every possible permutations.

74
00:08:04,389 --> 00:08:09,932
[SPEAKER_01]: There would be things that would still make him a bit kind of caught up and so on.

75
00:08:09,972 --> 00:08:22,697
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally, on that most base level, sotāpaṭṭhāna cannot be struck by that second arrow of mental anguish and turmoil.

76
00:08:24,077 --> 00:08:25,098
[SPEAKER_01]: And anybody can relate to it.

77
00:08:25,118 --> 00:08:32,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I'm sure everybody in their life has experienced something that the mind just keeps revolving around, something of the unpleasant kind.

78
00:08:33,622 --> 00:08:48,790
[SPEAKER_01]: They would keep you awake at night, they would make you worried, and eh... and they would kind of... it's enough for little things to just remind you of it, and you just couldn't... you... well, you can't help it, and you just find your mind already, fully,

79
00:08:50,593 --> 00:08:54,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Reaching towards it, trying to cancel it, trying to resolve it, trying to deny it.

80
00:08:55,115 --> 00:09:02,678
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you just have to go through until the mind tires out, and you get distracted with something, and then you just pray that nothing reminds the mind of that which already bothers it.

81
00:09:04,059 --> 00:09:09,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And when it does again, and again, and again...

82
00:09:09,221 --> 00:09:10,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of pretty much the nature of dukkha.

83
00:09:11,402 --> 00:09:15,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's stress, or anxiety, or traumas, or whatever, that's pretty much the common thread.

84
00:09:16,364 --> 00:09:18,505
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind just going back at it,

85
00:09:19,797 --> 00:09:21,518
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're suffering on account of it.

86
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:26,182
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you could, you would stop that mind.

87
00:09:26,262 --> 00:09:27,123
[SPEAKER_01]: You would tell it to stop.

88
00:09:28,123 --> 00:09:29,865
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that mind were to listen to you, it would stop.

89
00:09:30,905 --> 00:09:31,486
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't.

90
00:09:32,226 --> 00:09:34,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't... it doesn't understand your language, basically.

91
00:09:36,109 --> 00:09:40,192
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can tell it all you want, you can talk to it all you want, but it won't hear you.

92
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:42,614
[SPEAKER_01]: However, you can train it.

93
00:09:43,835 --> 00:09:48,819
[SPEAKER_01]: You can train it with basically the steps that the Buddha laid out.

94
00:09:49,957 --> 00:09:56,768
[SPEAKER_01]: You can contain it, you can train it, you can harness it, you can direct it, you can learn how to read its signs and tokens and indications.

95
00:09:59,732 --> 00:10:01,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, the simile of the cars...

96
00:10:02,852 --> 00:10:08,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Roaming around, everyone's pastures, eating unwarily, and being punished, and you being punished on account of it.

97
00:10:08,595 --> 00:10:09,395
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much it.

98
00:10:09,896 --> 00:10:13,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind just won't let go, just revolves, just spins, just spins.

99
00:10:14,018 --> 00:10:14,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Who said what?

100
00:10:14,838 --> 00:10:15,879
[SPEAKER_01]: And now what happened?

101
00:10:16,519 --> 00:10:18,240
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you suffer.

102
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:21,702
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't sleep at night, full of anxiety, full of restlessness, full of worry.

103
00:10:22,202 --> 00:10:23,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is a trigger now.

104
00:10:25,224 --> 00:10:28,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but if it only... if only I could stop thinking.

105
00:10:28,685 --> 00:10:31,427
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, again, thinking is the mind's domain.

106
00:10:32,343 --> 00:10:33,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking is what the mind does.

107
00:10:34,885 --> 00:10:35,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't stop that.

108
00:10:36,267 --> 00:10:38,329
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not your doing, so to speak.

109
00:10:39,810 --> 00:10:56,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You, sure, you can distract yourself, you can look away from it, but fundamentally, that mind, for as long as untrained, it will always carry the ability to turn against you, to turn against itself, to turn against anything that's felt if it deems unwanted.

110
00:10:57,628 --> 00:11:02,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will cause you suffering, that will cause you fear, pain, anxiety, stress, everything that follows.

111
00:11:04,335 --> 00:11:07,298
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you train it, pretty much, through the gradual training.

112
00:11:07,358 --> 00:11:15,526
[SPEAKER_01]: First, virtue, that contains the behavior, regardless of what your views are and where the emphasis is.

113
00:11:16,067 --> 00:11:17,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, sensory strain.

114
00:11:17,108 --> 00:11:17,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Again,

115
00:11:18,719 --> 00:11:21,503
[SPEAKER_01]: It might seem like an indirect thing to you.

116
00:11:21,784 --> 00:11:29,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, virtue and sense restraint does not bear direct relevance to my problem, to my trauma, to my stress, to my suffering.

117
00:11:29,853 --> 00:11:30,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

118
00:11:30,493 --> 00:11:40,655
[SPEAKER_01]: It bears... it doesn't bear direct connection to the specifics of your trauma, of your memories, of your experience, of your past.

119
00:11:41,155 --> 00:11:50,137
[SPEAKER_01]: But it bears a very direct connection to the wildness, wilderness of your own mind, which is the fundamental reason for your suffering.

120
00:11:50,317 --> 00:11:51,777
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not because of what happened to you.

121
00:11:52,757 --> 00:11:55,978
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the mind, by being an untrained entity,

122
00:11:58,527 --> 00:12:12,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Encountered something that made it very, kind of, eh... agitated, angry, eh... made it turn against you, against everything, made it want to deny all of it, and that's the utmost pain, fundamentally.

123
00:12:13,830 --> 00:12:25,413
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, the the simile from that sutta illustrates the point that even if the highway robbers are chopping your limbs, circumstances are not the cause of suffering, are not the cause of ill-will.

124
00:12:26,253 --> 00:12:31,778
[SPEAKER_01]: By the same proxy, there are no circumstances that are not caused of sensual desire either.

125
00:12:32,819 --> 00:12:48,752
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing then that keeping the precepts and guarding of the sense doors and watchfulness of your intentions, so, like, what do you want to do, why you want to do it, all those reflections we often talk about, yes, absolutely, bears no relevance, no direct connection

126
00:12:49,585 --> 00:12:55,927
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the simile, as we so often repeat it, of a wild elephant, you need to tame it, you need to train it, you need to make it obey you,

127
00:13:15,170 --> 00:13:22,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can try and talk to it and, you know, take it, get some counseling and psychotherapy, but again, it does not speak that language.

128
00:13:23,554 --> 00:13:25,495
[SPEAKER_01]: What you need to do is first catch it.

129
00:13:26,955 --> 00:13:33,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, stop allowing it to spill itself out into physical actions, into verbal actions.

130
00:13:33,598 --> 00:13:34,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Contain it.

131
00:13:34,819 --> 00:13:42,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it will kick and scream and urinate and defecate and rebel, but now it's tied down with the leather straps.

132
00:13:43,439 --> 00:13:54,387
[SPEAKER_01]: And when it comes down, when it realizes there is no compromise, I am not being let off the hook now, then, you... well, first of all, you would see that.

133
00:13:54,467 --> 00:14:02,353
[SPEAKER_01]: You would see now that the mind is not as rebellious against precepts, although it's been trying to find the ways to make you break it, to make you go against them.

134
00:14:03,234 --> 00:14:08,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, look, look, it has nothing to do with this or that, or with this anxiety, or with that anxiety.

135
00:14:08,358 --> 00:14:09,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it has to do with you.

136
00:14:10,639 --> 00:14:11,520
[SPEAKER_01]: And you are the reason.

137
00:14:12,090 --> 00:14:14,472
[SPEAKER_01]: that the circumstances cause me anxieties and so on.

138
00:14:14,812 --> 00:14:15,873
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is the reason.

139
00:14:17,995 --> 00:14:26,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to stop listening to these wild inclinations of your own mind, because that's why the mind remains pretty much in charge of you, not other way around.

140
00:14:27,202 --> 00:14:33,847
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once it's contained, once it's tamed in that sense, you then see like, oh, look, I got this mind trapped now.

141
00:14:34,927 --> 00:14:39,491
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a circumstance that it's unpleasant,

142
00:14:40,931 --> 00:14:44,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... and I saw the mind's reaction to it.

143
00:14:46,452 --> 00:14:49,573
[SPEAKER_01]: And I saw that, ah, that was why I used to suffer.

144
00:14:50,033 --> 00:15:05,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Because before, that mind that now I trapped, through perpetual virtue, establishment of virtue, aggregate of virtue, sense restraint, and pretty much 24-7 watchfulness of my intentions, and containing it,

145
00:15:06,407 --> 00:15:10,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, I see it now as an entity there, that reacts to this and that.

146
00:15:10,730 --> 00:15:16,855
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to before, where I didn't see any of that, where it was completely identified with me, with my point of view.

147
00:15:16,935 --> 00:15:27,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when something would... when circumstances would arise, and mind would kick and scream against it, I was just thinking, it's me who is kicking and screaming against it.

148
00:15:27,464 --> 00:15:31,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there was like no... no... no buffers on, nothing that I could see.

149
00:15:32,050 --> 00:15:36,255
[SPEAKER_01]: In between, it was just this, and then the complete overwhelming anxiety and fear.

150
00:15:36,756 --> 00:15:43,584
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, when the mind is trapped and contained, I actually circumsensitize, and I see the mind doing that, not me.

151
00:15:43,944 --> 00:15:44,705
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not doing it.

152
00:15:46,269 --> 00:15:47,870
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is trying to go against it.

153
00:15:48,230 --> 00:15:54,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, equally, well, once you start to see that, then even the mind kind of recognizes, okay, so I can't fool you anymore.

154
00:15:54,772 --> 00:16:03,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Before, when I did, when I did, like, sort of leap towards pleasure, or I resisted the pain, or I ignored the neutral feeling, you thought it was you.

155
00:16:03,634 --> 00:16:05,675
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was great, because I was invisible, basically.

156
00:16:07,295 --> 00:16:10,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you completely thought it's you and, oh, then I need to do this or need to do that.

157
00:16:11,036 --> 00:16:13,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely missing where the problem was.

158
00:16:13,177 --> 00:16:17,779
[SPEAKER_01]: And the problem was simply mind doing it to you, because you haven't trained it.

159
00:16:18,739 --> 00:16:24,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can hate your mind or something, but you wouldn't hate a wild elephant, because, well, that's all it knows.

160
00:16:25,461 --> 00:16:27,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Its wilderness is all it knows.

161
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:34,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would be kind of stupid to sort of feel ill-will towards that, in the same sense.

162
00:16:34,641 --> 00:16:39,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can't blame your mind for doing that, because that's what an untrained mind can only do.

163
00:16:42,483 --> 00:16:45,424
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of this understanding begins with sotāpatti.

164
00:16:46,545 --> 00:16:48,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the signs of your mind, i.e.

165
00:16:48,125 --> 00:16:51,746
[SPEAKER_01]: seeing your mind as an actual entity, as an actual phenomenon, there.

166
00:16:54,006 --> 00:16:55,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Through trapping it rightly,

167
00:16:56,213 --> 00:16:58,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Taming it, containing it, discerning the intentions.

168
00:16:59,956 --> 00:17:11,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully develop that training, so there are... so that there are no circumstances left, however extreme, that can still make you forget where the problem is.

169
00:17:11,667 --> 00:17:23,778
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you can't forget where the problem is, that mind will be completely then tamed, and there will be no kicking and screaming, regardless of what comes that way, and there will be no... there will be basically an incapability of suffering.

170
00:17:25,249 --> 00:17:26,005
[SPEAKER_00]: In your experience.

171
00:17:27,387 --> 00:17:41,519
[SPEAKER_00]: So you say you've contained the mind, that elephant in that boundary of precepts and so on, and then something, you know, that used to make it upset, or lustful, or angry, comes in its view.

172
00:17:42,279 --> 00:17:45,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So that elephant starts getting all roused up.

173
00:17:45,722 --> 00:17:47,083
[SPEAKER_01]: So what do you do?

174
00:17:47,503 --> 00:17:47,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

175
00:17:47,864 --> 00:17:50,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like, okay, so I'm keeping the precepts now.

176
00:17:50,766 --> 00:17:52,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Surely, I must be containing the mind.

177
00:17:52,628 --> 00:17:53,668
[SPEAKER_01]: There is something that's upsetting me.

178
00:17:53,708 --> 00:17:54,229
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do?

179
00:17:54,769 --> 00:17:55,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there's the point.

180
00:17:56,510 --> 00:17:59,552
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that whatever you're supposed to do is you're already doing it.

181
00:18:02,193 --> 00:18:02,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Trap your mind.

182
00:18:03,694 --> 00:18:06,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't let that wild animal go... get loose.

183
00:18:06,676 --> 00:18:07,556
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you need to do.

184
00:18:08,917 --> 00:18:10,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So, something upsetting, something challenging comes.

185
00:18:11,278 --> 00:18:12,119
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I deal with this?

186
00:18:12,739 --> 00:18:19,383
[SPEAKER_01]: I deal with this by not dealing with it, but by dealing with this mind remaining trapped.

187
00:18:20,363 --> 00:18:23,525
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I need to remain sort of vigilant of.

188
00:18:24,779 --> 00:18:26,599
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're kind of keeping it safe, in a way.

189
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:29,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're keeping it safe for your own sake.

190
00:18:30,040 --> 00:18:34,061
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't know what... and for its own sake, but the mind doesn't know what it's good for itself.

191
00:18:34,601 --> 00:18:35,382
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a wild animal.

192
00:18:36,862 --> 00:18:40,763
[SPEAKER_01]: But, even more, you are the one getting punished for its actions.

193
00:18:41,483 --> 00:18:46,645
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole simile of the cow herd and a shepherd that the Buddha gave.

194
00:18:48,305 --> 00:18:52,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, oh, yeah, it's like this mind does this and I'm the one who suffers.

195
00:18:53,973 --> 00:18:59,219
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, that... and that's again, like, people think sense restraint is just to avoid looking, avoid hearing.

196
00:18:59,259 --> 00:18:59,379
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

197
00:18:59,419 --> 00:19:02,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense restraint is restraining your senses, including your own mind.

198
00:19:02,722 --> 00:19:12,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you have these... you have something challenging comes up, you need to restrain yourself from trying to deal with it on that specific level.

199
00:19:13,632 --> 00:19:23,735
[SPEAKER_01]: And not lose perspective, that the problem is, it's the mind that gets loose, that escapes the enclosure, and then starts spinning and acting and doing, and then I suffer.

200
00:19:24,395 --> 00:19:42,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, if I refrain from... if I basically restrain my urge and impulse to, yeah, jump with that mind, and go and try to deal with seemingly what caused me suffering, but instead just remain watchful of that mind, so it's kept enclosed,

201
00:19:43,869 --> 00:19:44,569
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll stop suffering.

202
00:19:47,410 --> 00:19:49,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the challenge in the beginning.

203
00:19:49,371 --> 00:19:49,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So what do I do?

204
00:19:49,991 --> 00:19:50,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what you do.

205
00:19:50,852 --> 00:19:55,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't fall into a trap of trying to deal with things specifically thinking that's where the problem was.

206
00:19:56,774 --> 00:20:09,019
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually remain fully guarding that mind from escaping into the projected kind of idea of where the problem was.

207
00:20:09,399 --> 00:20:10,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you want attainment.

208
00:20:10,399 --> 00:20:11,580
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your sole goal.

209
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:40,167
[SPEAKER_01]: And everything that comes your way, or he said this, and we have this issue, or that issue, or I remember this, and my trauma of that, all of that is just to tempt you to give in and let that mind go, let that wild, untamed mind back into wilderness, either intentionally, volitionally, or kind of through lapsing of perspective.

210
00:20:41,149 --> 00:20:44,191
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of the issues are not really issues.

211
00:20:44,831 --> 00:20:50,515
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just there to distract you from what the real issue is, which is the wilderness of the mind.

212
00:20:52,196 --> 00:20:58,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have kept the precepts and if you practice the sensory strain, that will become more and more clear.

213
00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:00,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter what comes your way.

214
00:21:02,118 --> 00:21:09,800
[SPEAKER_01]: The more things come your way, the harder you turn towards that mind remaining locked and contained.

215
00:21:10,560 --> 00:21:20,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything then, basically, with the right perspective, everything becomes a reminder of where the emphasis should be, which is contained mind.

216
00:21:23,163 --> 00:21:24,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and eventually it...

217
00:21:26,641 --> 00:21:28,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you're breaking its evil spirit.

218
00:21:29,303 --> 00:21:30,564
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say that.

219
00:21:30,604 --> 00:21:33,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You're breaking the spirit that causes harm to itself and you.

220
00:21:50,837 --> 00:22:01,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, as a skilled trainer, you would have things and sounds and images and memories coming up, but your eyes remain on the animal.

221
00:22:01,646 --> 00:22:03,087
[SPEAKER_01]: You're watching its reactions.

222
00:22:03,408 --> 00:22:05,830
[SPEAKER_01]: You yourself are not directly concerned

223
00:22:06,270 --> 00:22:11,293
[SPEAKER_01]: With anything that your senses are coming across, including your own mind.

224
00:22:11,413 --> 00:22:15,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's all around, but you are keeping the eyes on that contained mind.

225
00:22:16,836 --> 00:22:17,857
[SPEAKER_01]: And see its reaction.

226
00:22:17,937 --> 00:22:19,358
[SPEAKER_01]: See if... is it flinching?

227
00:22:19,458 --> 00:22:20,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it... is it trying to react?

228
00:22:21,559 --> 00:22:25,941
[SPEAKER_01]: And pick those signs and recognize that there is still room for improvement.

229
00:22:26,121 --> 00:22:32,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, no, look, it's completely calm, and it's just looking back at me, and it's paying no attention to these things, that mind.

230
00:22:33,918 --> 00:22:34,478
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know.

231
00:22:34,598 --> 00:22:37,419
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the simile of the cows and the shepherd.

232
00:22:37,779 --> 00:22:39,740
[SPEAKER_01]: When he recognizes them, he can even relax.

233
00:22:40,080 --> 00:22:41,501
[SPEAKER_01]: He can go to sleep under a tree.

234
00:22:41,641 --> 00:22:43,261
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows it will not go too far.

235
00:22:43,401 --> 00:22:44,182
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not react.

236
00:22:44,742 --> 00:22:53,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the simile of the battle elephant, where eventually, when has been properly trained, even when it's taken amidst into the core of the battle,

237
00:22:54,385 --> 00:23:03,536
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's being prodded with spears and loud drums and screams and stuff, it doesn't flinch, it doesn't move, it does not try to sort of, you know, shy away.

238
00:23:03,756 --> 00:23:07,020
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just there, kind of resilient to all of that.

239
00:23:08,402 --> 00:23:10,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, then the mind becomes as pliable.

240
00:23:11,105 --> 00:23:15,148
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, you can, you can tell it to do whatever you want it to do.

241
00:23:15,248 --> 00:23:18,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have learned how to speak to it, so to speak.

242
00:23:18,191 --> 00:23:20,253
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be trying to just stop it, stop it.

243
00:23:20,313 --> 00:23:22,975
[SPEAKER_01]: But you know, you know that if you do this, mind does that.

244
00:23:23,576 --> 00:23:25,357
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do it like this, the mind does something else.

245
00:23:26,058 --> 00:23:34,221
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have learned, you have found the ways of communicating directly to it, which is an indirect way from where you are when you start practicing.

246
00:23:34,261 --> 00:23:36,903
[SPEAKER_01]: When you start practicing, everything is in front, direct.

247
00:23:37,823 --> 00:23:41,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Objective experience, my mind, intentions, wanting to do this, not wanting to do that.

248
00:23:42,025 --> 00:23:45,206
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, mind does not respond to that.

249
00:23:45,306 --> 00:23:48,067
[SPEAKER_01]: First, it needs to be contained indirectly, caught up.

250
00:23:48,347 --> 00:23:49,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, wait until it calms down.

251
00:23:50,930 --> 00:23:55,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, through sensory strength and so on, you get to learn its signs and features.

252
00:23:55,394 --> 00:23:58,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what does it want to do when pleasure is present?

253
00:23:58,956 --> 00:24:00,898
[SPEAKER_01]: What does it want to do when pain is present?

254
00:24:02,119 --> 00:24:02,999
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it inclined to?

255
00:24:03,020 --> 00:24:06,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it... Do I just allow it to welcome it, relish it, think about it more?

256
00:24:08,043 --> 00:24:10,345
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, yeah, but see... And you can catch yourself.

257
00:24:10,365 --> 00:24:11,246
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not going to be perfect.

258
00:24:11,266 --> 00:24:12,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, pleasure comes and...

259
00:24:13,470 --> 00:24:18,131
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have lapsed, and you're sort of proliferating and fantasizing, and then you catch yourself, and again, you have a choice.

260
00:24:18,231 --> 00:24:20,572
[SPEAKER_01]: When you catch yourself, it's like, oh, I must stop.

261
00:24:20,672 --> 00:24:23,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you must stop, but also you need to know why you're stopping.

262
00:24:24,773 --> 00:24:26,773
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like, okay, so I can continue this.

263
00:24:27,593 --> 00:24:38,396
[SPEAKER_01]: I can continue allowing and and fueling these thoughts further myself, but I should not forget that that is the direct cause for the mind still being untamed.

264
00:24:39,916 --> 00:24:42,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, when that untamed mind

265
00:24:43,097 --> 00:24:56,544
[SPEAKER_01]: When me, paired with the attained mind, untamed mind, encounter something in the world of a displeasing kind, well, that mind will probably turn against me, turn against itself, it will cause trouble, and I will be the one feeling pain.

266
00:24:58,265 --> 00:25:01,727
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the most direct method for mental training.

267
00:25:01,747 --> 00:25:12,192
[SPEAKER_01]: For, like, you can practice concentration all you want, focusing all you want, but all of that is making you... it's not making you any more aware of your mind of that wild animal.

268
00:25:14,059 --> 00:25:26,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you recognize that when you catch yourself in these unwholesome behaviors, you sort of remember, recollect the context of and the connection between the two.

269
00:25:27,230 --> 00:25:30,953
[SPEAKER_01]: The connection being, this behavior, I can handle it.

270
00:25:31,653 --> 00:25:32,814
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't do anything to me.

271
00:25:33,475 --> 00:25:37,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But it fuels the wilderness of my own mind.

272
00:25:38,839 --> 00:25:42,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, as I already know, on account of my limited experience,

273
00:25:43,816 --> 00:25:57,879
[SPEAKER_01]: That mind, when it doesn't get what it wants, when it gets what it doesn't want, when it's separated from the things it wants to be united with, when it's united with things it wants to be separate from, I'm the one suffering on account of it.

274
00:25:59,839 --> 00:26:12,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have a choice to continue following my desires, to continue ignoring my intentions, and all of that will maintain the wilderness of that mind, which means all of that will keep me paired with suffering.

275
00:26:13,662 --> 00:26:27,312
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I don't then do these things, and keep containing the mind, keep taming it through that containment, and not providing unsuitable food for it, the mind will stop reacting in the ways that cause me suffering.

276
00:26:27,412 --> 00:26:29,053
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, I will be free from suffering.

277
00:26:31,795 --> 00:26:37,740
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you confirm that for yourself, the inability to suffer, because the mind cannot do

278
00:26:39,143 --> 00:26:43,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Those wild things anymore cannot crave against the pain, cannot crave towards pleasure.

279
00:26:45,870 --> 00:26:48,873
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you know, okay, this is the beginning of the right view.

280
00:26:49,554 --> 00:26:51,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no second arrow following.

281
00:26:51,896 --> 00:26:53,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I'm hiding behind the tree.

282
00:26:54,319 --> 00:26:58,443
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just because the first arrow struck and the animal, my mind, is unmoved.

283
00:27:00,057 --> 00:27:03,138
[SPEAKER_01]: And it struck again on another occasion, and it was still unmoved.

284
00:27:03,998 --> 00:27:07,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Struck again on another occasion, and it was again unmoved.

285
00:27:07,859 --> 00:27:10,340
[SPEAKER_01]: By that time, you know, okay, it's not gonna move.

286
00:27:10,640 --> 00:27:16,122
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start to develop confidence in your own training that has been successful, because the animal is not moving.

287
00:27:16,642 --> 00:27:18,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you wouldn't even have to know.

288
00:27:18,802 --> 00:27:20,723
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, you might be still a bit doubtful.

289
00:27:21,691 --> 00:27:22,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, will it move?

290
00:27:22,291 --> 00:27:22,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Will it not?

291
00:27:23,172 --> 00:27:27,273
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it doesn't move, number of times, even that doubt would just evaporate.

292
00:27:27,814 --> 00:27:31,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will not be able to doubt what's already there, unmoving.

293
00:27:31,875 --> 00:27:36,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, you realize, that your mind realized for itself.

294
00:27:38,078 --> 00:27:39,398
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you know that you know.

295
00:27:39,759 --> 00:27:44,060
[SPEAKER_00]: You know that the mind knows now.

296
00:27:44,741 --> 00:27:46,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what the Sutta described.

297
00:27:46,381 --> 00:27:49,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, arahant, he knows that he knows.

298
00:27:50,808 --> 00:27:52,428
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just he had a knowledge of something.

299
00:27:52,508 --> 00:27:53,769
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows that he knows.

300
00:27:54,949 --> 00:27:57,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like a double, two-tier knowledge.

301
00:27:57,770 --> 00:27:58,830
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what it is.

302
00:27:59,590 --> 00:28:02,511
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that your mind now knows for itself.

303
00:28:03,511 --> 00:28:13,234
[SPEAKER_01]: You've been training it and now you know that that mind has seen for itself that this is more beneficial than what it used to do when it was wild.

304
00:28:18,458 --> 00:28:22,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I said, again, just to repeat, basically, that's the... it all boils down to...

305
00:28:25,241 --> 00:28:30,343
[SPEAKER_01]: If you keep the precepts, like the Buddha tells us how to communicate with the mind, right from the start.

306
00:28:31,004 --> 00:28:32,224
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do I become sotāpanna?

307
00:28:32,244 --> 00:28:35,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by practicing the gradual training, which starts with the patujana.

308
00:28:37,727 --> 00:28:46,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep the precepts, guard the sense doors, see the danger in the slightest fault, watchful, be watchful of your intentions,

309
00:28:48,222 --> 00:28:50,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Actions, things you delight in.

310
00:28:51,304 --> 00:28:53,606
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you're speaking to your mind already.

311
00:28:55,407 --> 00:28:56,367
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you don't know that.

312
00:28:57,868 --> 00:29:03,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you still don't see the mind, you already started communicating certain things, certain information.

313
00:29:03,972 --> 00:29:08,696
[SPEAKER_01]: You already started communicating with that mind by developing gradual training.

314
00:29:09,156 --> 00:29:15,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the time will come when you're gonna catch on and you will recognize, oh, yeah, the mind is actually now

315
00:29:16,370 --> 00:29:30,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Becoming more pliable, because of everything I've been doing, before the aggregate of virtue that I developed, watchfulness of my intentions, all of that, oh, that's why now I can see this mind not running away as before, not acting as wild as before.

316
00:29:32,685 --> 00:29:38,250
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can carry on speaking to it in the same manner, but now you know for yourself.

317
00:29:39,340 --> 00:29:47,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you see how what you're doing is directly communicating certain intentions, certain meanings, certain information to that mind.

318
00:29:55,770 --> 00:30:00,653
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, that's like the... kind of has another layer, that analogy of the language.

319
00:30:00,713 --> 00:30:06,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Like we said, it's like you speak English, but your mind speaks the sign language, for example.

320
00:30:07,788 --> 00:30:10,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're speaking English and waving your arms.

321
00:30:10,929 --> 00:30:20,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind does not hear what you're saying, but it picks on you waving arms and, you know, the whole energy, your sort of output of it.

322
00:30:20,494 --> 00:30:22,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't even know what you're telling through it.

323
00:30:22,474 --> 00:30:24,335
[SPEAKER_01]: All you might be saying is, calm down, calm down.

324
00:30:24,715 --> 00:30:27,557
[SPEAKER_01]: What you're actually saying it is craving, craving, more craving.

325
00:30:27,797 --> 00:30:29,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

326
00:30:29,357 --> 00:30:30,458
[SPEAKER_01]: And the mind reacts to that.

327
00:30:32,125 --> 00:30:39,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And basically, you don't even know that you are fueling it, because you don't know what communication sign the mind picks on.

328
00:30:39,712 --> 00:30:40,072
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop it.

329
00:30:40,092 --> 00:30:40,432
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop it.

330
00:30:40,452 --> 00:30:40,873
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop it.

331
00:30:40,893 --> 00:30:41,153
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop it.

332
00:30:41,173 --> 00:30:44,876
[SPEAKER_02]: You're communicating the opposite of what...

333
00:30:45,316 --> 00:30:45,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

334
00:30:45,877 --> 00:30:46,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the opposite.

335
00:30:46,517 --> 00:30:47,758
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I call... but just stop.

336
00:30:47,818 --> 00:30:49,199
[SPEAKER_01]: I just want this mind to stop.

337
00:30:49,240 --> 00:30:51,461
[SPEAKER_01]: I just want this thinking to stop causing me suffering.

338
00:30:52,022 --> 00:30:54,284
[SPEAKER_01]: But your whole intention behind it

339
00:30:57,221 --> 00:31:03,026
[SPEAKER_01]: The sign language of that intention, the movements of that intention, are telling your mind just go wild with it.

340
00:31:03,967 --> 00:31:07,169
[SPEAKER_01]: That craving you've been doing more, well, here, I'm adding more fuel for it.

341
00:31:08,651 --> 00:31:22,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, until you learn first for yourself, where the emphasis should be, so it's... and that's... see, the point there is that the emphasis for everybody is like, oh, what I want, and what I choose, and what's right in front of me.

342
00:31:23,993 --> 00:31:24,573
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want this.

343
00:31:24,633 --> 00:31:25,314
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want that.

344
00:31:25,854 --> 00:31:27,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this case, I want mine to stop.

345
00:31:27,635 --> 00:31:27,955
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't...

346
00:31:28,095 --> 00:31:28,715
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm telling it.

347
00:31:28,815 --> 00:31:29,796
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm wishing for it.

348
00:31:30,636 --> 00:31:31,717
[SPEAKER_01]: But all that is irrelevant.

349
00:31:32,697 --> 00:31:36,779
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is just you uttering words that the mind doesn't understand.

350
00:31:37,339 --> 00:31:47,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of you wishing for the mind to become peaceful and trying to really concentrate into the peace and so on, keep the precepts, guard the sense doors,

